Name the fault that a guard-page abort was hiding

When the spill runs into a guard page, the abort it raises is a second
exception: the frame that was being filled is left one arm_context_t above the
new one, with the real pc/far/esr in it (see nio-fleh-early-fault.patch), and
the panic prints only the spill.  Every round of this port has thus reported
'the vector faulted' instead of what the kernel was doing.

Print the abandoned frame before the panic: gap, both addresses and the cpu's
intstack top go on one line, and every value is range-checked first so a wild
sp degrades to no line instead of a second fault.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c b/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
index 2cc442e..e5e1e40 100644
--- a/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
@@ -466,6 +466,33 @@ panic_with_thread_kernel_state(const char *msg, arm_saved_state_t *ss)
 	ss_valid = is_saved_state64(ss);
 	arm_saved_state64_t *state = saved_state64(ss);
 
+	/*
+	 * nio: an abort taken while the previous frame was still being spilled
+	 * leaves that older frame exactly one context above this one, and the
+	 * older frame is the fault that actually stopped the boot (the spill
+	 * only ran into a guard page because the stack was already past its
+	 * end).  nio-fleh-early-fault.patch makes pc/far/esr readable there.
+	 * The gap and the range are checked first so a wild sp cannot turn a
+	 * panic into a second fault.
+	 */
+	if (ss_valid) {
+		uintptr_t prev = (uintptr_t)state->sp;
+		uintptr_t here = (uintptr_t)state;
+		if (prev > here && (prev - here) == 0x350 &&
+		    prev >= (uintptr_t)gVirtBase && prev < (uintptr_t)static_memory_end &&
+		    (prev & 7) == 0) {
+			uint64_t prev_pc = *(volatile uint64_t *)(prev + 0x108);
+			uint64_t prev_far = *(volatile uint64_t *)(prev + 0x118);
+			uint32_t prev_esr = *(volatile uint32_t *)(prev + 0x120);
+			if (prev_pc >= (uintptr_t)gVirtBase && prev_pc < (uintptr_t)static_memory_end) {
+				kprintf("nio: nested abort, previous frame pc=%llx far=%llx esr=%x sp=%llx istk=%llx\n",
+				    (unsigned long long)prev_pc, (unsigned long long)prev_far, prev_esr,
+				    (unsigned long long)(prev + 0x350),
+				    (unsigned long long)(uintptr_t)getCpuDatap()->intstack_top);
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	os_atomic_cmpxchg(&original_faulting_state, NULL, state, seq_cst);
 
 	// rdar://80659177
